.TH TRAIL-PLUGIN 5 2026-10-17 "Peios trail 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
trail-plugin \- Trail plugin process interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
[plugins.<name>]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Some content is not written but derived: API reference extracted from source
comments, tables generated from a schema. A plugin block asks an external
command to produce that content at build time, so the page and its source can
never disagree.
.PP
.PP
.nf
```{kernel-doc} drivers/base/core.c
:export:
```
.fi
.PP
The fence's info string is \fB{name}\fR, which is how
MyST spells a directive. Trail hands
the block to the command the site configures under that name, and puts the
markdown that comes back where the fence was.
.PP
.SS "Writing a block"
.PP
.PP
.nf
```{name} argument
:option: value
:flag:

body
```
.fi
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB{name}\fR\fR names a plugin configured in the root \fBtrail.toml\fR. A
\fB{name}\fR
  that is not configured is a \fBbuild error\fR, the same as a mistyped
  admonition marker. Otherwise it would render as a code block, and nobody
  notices a code block that should have been a page of reference.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe argument\fR is everything after \fB{name}\fR on the opening line. What
it
  means is up to the plugin.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOptions\fR are \fB:key: value\fR lines at the start of the block. A flag
has no
  value. An indented line continues the previous option's value, and a
  trailing \fB\e\fR on a piece is dropped, as in reStructuredText. Giving the
same
  option twice is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe body\fR is whatever follows the options, after one optional blank line.
.PP
A plugin block works anywhere a fenced code block does: inside list items and
blockquotes, with backticks or tildes, at any fence length. To show the syntax
on a page, as this one does, wrap it in a longer fence: the inner fence is
then content, not a block. Indented code blocks are never plugin blocks.
.PP
An unclosed plugin fence is an error, because it would otherwise take the rest
of the page as its body.
.PP
.SS "Configuring a plugin"
.PP
Plugins are declared in the root \fBtrail.toml\fR, one table each:
.PP
.PP
.nf
[plugins.kernel-doc]
command = ["python3", "../tools/docs/trail-kernel-doc/trail-kernel-doc", "--srctree", ".."]
.fi
.PP
\fBcommand\fR is the program and its arguments, run directly, not through a
shell.
Two more keys, \fBanchors\fR and \fBafter\fR, let a plugin
link to the rest of the site. A program
containing a \fB/\fR is a path relative to the site root and must exist. A
bare
name is looked up on \fBPATH\fR. The working directory is the site root.
.PP
A plugin configured in a
merged tree's \fBtrail.toml\fR treats
that tree's root as the site root throughout: its program path, working
directory, \fBroot\fR and \fBfile\fR are all relative to the tree, exactly as
when the
tree is built alone. Its blocks can still appear on any page of the site.
.PP
Markdown can only _name_ a configured plugin; it can never say what to run.
Building a site runs only the commands its \fBtrail.toml\fR lists.
.PP
Plugin names are lowercase letters, digits, \fB-\fR, and \fB_\fR. The
\fBtrail.toml\fR reference has the table's details.
.PP
.SS "What a plugin receives and returns"
.PP
Trail runs each plugin \fBat most once per build\fR, passing every one of its
blocks on
the site in a single request. A plugin with no blocks is not run at all. A
plugin that cross-references its own output can therefore see everything at
once: a function name in one block can link to the page where another block
documents that function.
.PP
The request arrives as JSON on stdin:
.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  "protocol": 1,
  "plugin": "kernel-doc",
  "root": "/home/me/kernel/docs",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "page": "/kernel/driver-api/core",
      "reference": "kernel/driver-api/core",
      "file": "kernel.product/2--driver-api.topic/1--core.md",
      "line": 14,
      "argument": "drivers/base/core.c",
      "options": { "export": "" },
      "body": ""
    }
  ]
}
.fi
.PP
.TP
\fBprotocol\fR
The protocol version, \fB1\fR. A plugin should refuse one it does not know.
.TP
\fBroot\fR
The site root, absolute.
.TP
\fBpage\fR
The URL path of the page the block is on.
.TP
\fBreference\fR
The same page as a \fB~\fR reference without the \fB~\fR. It is fully
qualified, so \fB~\fR + \fBreference\fR always resolves, and a plugin can link
back to a block's page with \fB~reference#anchor\fR.
.TP
\fBfile\fR, \fBline\fR
The block's source file, relative to the root, and the line its opening fence
is on. These are for the plugin's own messages.
.TP
\fBargument\fR, \fBoptions\fR, \fBbody\fR
The block, parsed.
.PP
The plugin answers on stdout:
.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  "blocks": [
    { "id": 0, "markdown": "### `device_add()`\en\enFunction documentation." }
  ],
  "depends_on": ["../drivers/base/core.c", "../include/linux/device.h"]
}
.fi
.PP
Every block id must be answered \fBexactly once\fR, each answer with **exactly
one** of \fBmarkdown\fR or \fBerror\fR. \fBdepends_on\fR lists the files the
output was
derived from, relative to the root or absolute.
.PP
A plugin that scans a directory, rather than reading files it was told about,
should list the directory as well as the files it read. A directory in
\fBdepends_on\fR stands for the set of files beneath it, so a file added there
or
removed counts as a change. Edits to those files count only through the files'
own entries.
.PP
The plugin's stderr is captured and replayed after execution, including on
cache hits. Use it for diagnostic messages.
.PP
.SS "Linking to the rest of the site"
.PP
A plugin sees only its own blocks. To link to something another page defines,
such as a function documented by hand, it can ask for the site's anchors with
\fBanchors = true\fR in its table. Its request then carries one more field:
every
page's explicit anchors, keyed by reference.
.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  "anchors": {
    "kernel/userspace/media/vidioc-querycap": ["c-v4l2_capability", "rst-vidioc-querycap"]
  }
}
.fi
.PP
\fBExplicit anchors\fR are the ids written in raw HTML, \fB<a id="...">\fR,
and
citation anchors. Heading ids are left out: Trail derives them from the
heading's words, so they change when a heading is reworded and are no basis
for generated links.
.PP
The anchors come from the pages as written, before any plugin's output is
added, so what one plugin generates never depends on another having run. To
link to targets that another plugin generates, name it in \fBafter\fR:
.PP
.PP
.nf
[plugins.kernel-include]
command = ["python3", "../tools/docs/trail-kernel-doc/trail-kernel-include", "--srctree", ".."]
anchors = true
after = ["kernel-doc"]
.fi
.PP
Trail then runs \fBkernel-doc\fR first and adds the anchors in its output to
those
\fBkernel-include\fR receives. Each plugin runs after every plugin its
\fBafter\fR
names. Independent plugins can run together in bounded groups; their results
and diagnostics retain dependency order, then name order. See
Build Cache for worker limits. \fBafter\fR without
\fBanchors\fR, a name that is not a configured plugin, and a cycle all fail
the
build.
.PP
The anchors are part of what a plugin's cached output depends on, so a new
anchor on any page reruns the plugins that receive anchors.
.PP
.SS "When something goes wrong"
.PP
All of these fail the build:
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
a plugin exits non-zero;
.IP \(bu 2
it prints something that is not a valid response;
.IP \(bu 2
it misses or repeats a block;
.IP \(bu 2
it answers a block with \fBerror\fR.
.PP
Block errors and malformed fences are collected across the whole site and
reported together, each against its source line:
.PP
.PP
.nf
2 plugin blocks failed:
  kernel.product/2--driver-api.topic/1--core.md:14: kernel-doc: drivers/base/nope.c does not exist
  kernel.product/2--driver-api.topic/3--bus.md:40: '{kernldoc}' fence names no configured plugin (configured: kernel-doc)
.fi
.PP
.SS "Generated content is ordinary content"
.PP
Expansion happens \fBwhen the site loads\fR, before anything reads a page. The
markdown a plugin returns then behaves exactly as if it had been typed:
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
its headings and raw HTML \fBid\fRs are anchors that links can target, and its
  own \fB~\fR links are checked during every build;
.IP \(bu 2
it counts toward reading time, unless the page's frontmatter sets
  \fBreading_minutes\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
it is indexed for search and appears in \fB/print\fR bundles;
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.link\fR aliases of the page carry it too.
.PP
That includes the markdown mirrors: a
mirror shows the expanded content, not the fence. Readers of the mirror,
people and machines alike, get the documentation rather than an instruction to
go and generate it.
.PP
Expanded output is not expanded again. A \fB{name}\fR fence inside a plugin's
output stays a plain code block.
.PP
To keep the output separate from the text around it, Trail sets it off with
blank lines. Inside a blockquote or a list item, every output line gets the
container's prefix.
.PP
.SS "Rebuilds and caching"
.PP
Trail keeps each plugin's last successful output. It reuses that output as
long as all of these are unchanged:
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
the plugin's blocks: arguments, options, bodies, and the pages they are on.
  A line-number change reruns a plugin that emitted diagnostics.
.IP \(bu 2
everything in its \fBdepends_on\fR. Files are compared by content, and
  directories by the names of the files beneath them, skipping names that
  start with a dot (such as editors' swap files). The resolved executable also
counts, including a bare command found on
  \fBPATH\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
for a plugin with \fBanchors = true\fR, the anchors it receives.
.PP
Under \fBtrail serve\fR, a dependency outside
the site root is watched as well, so editing a source file the plugin reads
rebuilds the site. A change reruns the whole plugin, not the affected blocks,
because the plugin can cross-reference across all of them.
.PP
The cache persists between processes in \fB.trail-cache/\fR. Use
\fB--no-cache\fR
when inputs such as network state or time affect a plugin. See
Build Cache for invalidation and worker limits.
.SS "Response validation"
.PP
The host rejects unknown fields in response objects and response block
objects.
Each \fBid\fR is an integer from zero to the number of request blocks minus
one.
The response contains one result for every request id, in any order.
Each result has one non-null string value in \fBmarkdown\fR or \fBerror\fR;
the other field is absent or null. \fBdepends_on\fR defaults to an empty list.
An empty successful Markdown string removes the fence.
The host does not recursively expand generated plugin fences.
The protocol version is 1. A plugin rejects versions it cannot interpret.
Plugin-specific arguments, option names, and body syntax belong to that
plugin.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trail (1),
.BR trail.toml (5)
.PP
